is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No fault found at the time of investigation. Both Campus 2 access VLANs (10.5.0.0/24 and 10.6.0.0/24) are present on switch4’s uplink and their default gateways are reachable.

Affected component: None. The switch uplink trunk and the gateway bridge interfaces are operating normally.
Mechanism: If a VLAN “fell off” the trunk, hosts on that VLAN would fail to ARP or ping their default gateway. Instead, multiple hosts in each subnet successfully reach their gateways with 0% loss (e.g., pc1→10.5.0.254, pc2→10.5.0.254, dc_pc5→10.5.0.254; pc3→10.6.0.254, pc4→10.6.0.254).
Localization: On switch4, the uplink interface eth0 carries 802.1Q subinterfaces eth0.5 and eth0.6, which are UP and bridged into br_vlan5 and br_vlan6 respectively. Access ports are correctly enslaved to those bridges (e.g., eth1/eth2/eth3→vlan5; eth4/eth5→vlan6). On the upstream sshswitch, eth0.5 and eth0.6 are UP and bridged into br_v5 (10.5.0.254) and br_v6 (10.6.0.254), and both gateway IPs are present and responding.
Root cause: Not reproducible; no evidence of a missing VLAN or trunk misconfiguration at present.
Corrective fix: No changes required. If users still report issues, check for transient physical disturbances during maintenance, end-host mispatching, or stale ARP/DHCP on clients. The trunk is correctly carrying VLANs 5 and 6, and gateways are healthy.

=== END ===